 1. Architecture and strategy (read from your docs first)                                                                        
                                                                                                                                 
 The tensor beat the tensor-in consumer wins is the ellipse. One direction + one scalar reproduces hatching, LIC, and Coherent   
 Line Drawing — Kang's ETF is exactly direction-only, so a Kuwahara variant is the known full-matrix consumer shape (Kyprianidis 
 et al. (https://www.kyprianidis.com/p/pg2009/jkyprian-pg2009.pdf), sector ellipse from λ₁/λ₂ + orientation). Your strategy note 
 already reached the same conclusion for row 12. The honest architecture question is where eigenvalues get computed: it can't    
 happen in a zero-new-UI consumer pass without state_param-driven decode (raw tensor in, compute λ₁λ₂ in-pixel — ~1 sqrt). My    
 recommendation: settle it once, centrally, and build only full-ellipse consumers per your 2026-10-16 decision.                  
                                                                                                                                 
 Axis-flow is a view. Kang's ETF (https://www.cs.princeton.edu/courses/archive/spring16/cos426/papers/Kang09.pdf) is the closest 
 published "axis field" concept; it's direction-only. Anything that consumes only the θ axis is a view module, not a             
 tensor-native one.                                                                                                              
                                                                                                                                 
 Failure modes to design for now: flat regions (trace≈0 → isotropic fallback → consumers must gate on trace), coherence-noise    
 (per Frangi (https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/BFb0056195.pdf), the eigenvalue-ratio measures are only stable above 
 an energy floor — same reason tensormap's coherence is "noisy where trace is very small"), flicker (per-frame tensor jitter →   
 the temporal smoother is a prerequisite for video), angle-doubling (all consumers must decode via atan2, measured safe incl.    
 half-angle in your Q6 tests).                                                                                                   
                                                                                                                                 
